WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.140
<v Person 1>Okay, so now we're getting</v>

2
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.520
into data warehousing.

3
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.415
And for this we have Amazon Redshift.

4
00:00:04.415 --> 00:00:08.070
So Redshift, as you may know, it is based on PostgreSQL,

5
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.234
but it is not used for OLTP.

6
00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.517
OLTP is online transaction processing.

7
00:00:12.517 --> 00:00:15.510
And RDS is a much better choice for this.

8
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.130
For Redshift, it is OLAP.

9
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.590
So online analytical processing,

10
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.380
and this is obviously when you want to do, analytics

11
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.150
and data warehousing.

12
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.890
The idea with Redshift, the cell point,

13
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.780
at least it like it has better performance

14
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.360
than other data warehouses and it can scale

15
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.430
to petabytes of data.

16
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.310
The particularity of Redshift is that the data is not stored

17
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.350
as a row, it is stored as a column.

18
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.358
So it's called a columnar storage of data.

19
00:00:39.358 --> 00:00:42.000
And the reason it is so is that if you want, for example,

20
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.850
to sum or do the average of a column,

21
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.950
then it can read all the values at once.

22
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.293
And so it's very efficient in that regards.

23
00:00:48.293 --> 00:00:52.950
So this is why Redshift is great for OLAP and not OLTP.

24
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.820
There is a engine called Massively Parallel Query Execution

25
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.490
or MPP, which allows you to perform queries

26
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.599
and distribute them across

27
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:03.390
so many Redshift nodes in your cluster

28
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.370
and get the best performance out of it.

29
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.010
You now have two modes to run your Redshift cluster.

30
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.460
You can have a provision cluster, which is on demand servers

31
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.440
that you provision ahead of time,

32
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.570
or now you have a serverless cluster.

33
00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.565
If you don't want to manage any of the infrastructure,

34
00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:21.090
it has a SQL interface for performing the queries.

35
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.012
So it is standard

36
00:01:22.012 --> 00:01:25.514
and you can plug in some tools such as AWS QuickSight,

37
00:01:25.514 --> 00:01:28.740
or Tableau to integrate with Redshift

38
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.390
and create some dashboards.

39
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.270
And Redshift will be running the queries very, very quickly.

40
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.285
So your dashboards are very responsive.

41
00:01:35.285 --> 00:01:39.090
Okay, so Redshift, how do we load data into it?

42
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.190
Well, number one, you can load it from S3

43
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.600
using a copy command.

44
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.250
We can use Kinesis' data Firehose

45
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.893
to load data into Redshift near real time.

46
00:01:46.893 --> 00:01:49.140
We can integrate with DynamoDB

47
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.420
or use DMS database migration service

48
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.160
to load data into Redshift.

49
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.480
Cluster can be really, really big.

50
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.220
So you can have hundreds of nodes.

51
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.320
Okay, the number is 100 plus.

52
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.780
It can change over time, obviously it can increase

53
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.590
and each node can have up to 16 terabytes of space.

54
00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.600
And again, that number could increase over time.

55
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.720
But what this means is that you can get really massive scale

56
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.743
for your Redshift clusters.

57
00:02:11.743 --> 00:02:14.010
You can provision multiple nodes

58
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.960
and you have single AZ support

59
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.880
for most of Redshift clusters.

60
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.740
But for some types of cluster,

61
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.410
you now have a multi AZ option,

62
00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.210
which gives you failover capabilities

63
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.943
in case you experience a disaster in one specific AZ.

64
00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.090
So you have two types of node in Redshift,

65
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.710
you have the leader node, which is here

66
00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.880
for doing query planning

67
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.120
and aggregate any results from the compute node.

68
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.070
And the compute node are gonna be here

69
00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.360
for performing the queries

70
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.020
and they're going to send the results back to the leader.

71
00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.450
Redshift is a managed service and therefore we get backup

72
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.950
and restore facility.

73
00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.450
It's launched within the VPC.

74
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.240
So we have VPC enhancements, IAM security

75
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.780
for accessing the cluster, KMS security for encryption

76
00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.204
of the data and monitoring through CloudWatch.

77
00:02:57.204 --> 00:02:58.260
There is this thing

78
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.170
called the Redshift enhanced VPC routing, which is

79
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.900
to make sure that your copy and unload goes directly

80
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.100
through the VPC.

81
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.510
So it gives you better performance and lower cost.

82
00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.900
Finally, remember, Redshift is something you have

83
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.983
to provisioned, so it's only worth it

84
00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:15.180
when you have a sustained usage of your cluster.

85
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.946
If your queries are sporadic instead, Athena is going

86
00:03:17.946 --> 00:03:20.760
to be a much greater, better choice.

87
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.680
Okay, now let's talk about

88
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.960
from an architectural perspective, the snapshots

89
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.250
and disaster recovery.

90
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.530
This is very important, you know this going into the exam.

91
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.990
So snapshots are going to be point in time backups

92
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.340
of a cluster and they're going

93
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.980
to be internally stored into Amazon S3.

94
00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.600
And anytime you redo a snapshot,

95
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.950
it's going to be incremental.

96
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.270
So only what has changed

97
00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.280
in your Redshift cluster will be saved.

98
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.890
And the cool thing is that you can restore a snapshot

99
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.930
into a new cluster.

100
00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.940
So it's not an old cluster

101
00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.260
that you can restore a snapshot into.

102
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.780
You have to create a new cluster from a snapshot.

103
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.060
So we have two kinds of snapshots.

104
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.980
The first one are automated snapshots,

105
00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.685
and they happen every eight hours

106
00:03:58.685 --> 00:04:00.875
or every five gigabytes of data is changed in your cluster

107
00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:02.636
or on the schedule that you set.

108
00:04:02.636 --> 00:04:04.920
And you're going to set a retention for your snapshot,

109
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.180
for example, 30 days.

110
00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.830
And after 30 days, your snapshots

111
00:04:08.743 --> 00:04:10.242
will be automatically removed.

112
00:04:10.242 --> 00:04:12.515
And then if you want to have very long term storage

113
00:04:12.515 --> 00:04:13.980
of your data for as a snapshot,

114
00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.570
then you create a manual snapshot.

115
00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.150
And in that case, the manual snapshot is retained

116
00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.299
until you delete it.

117
00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:22.980
So this is very, very similar to how RDS works.

118
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.020
There's one cool thing though with Redshift is

119
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.490
that you can configure Redshift

120
00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.699
to automatically copy your snapshots either automated

121
00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:32.880
or manual to another AWS region.

122
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.995
And that is really, really cool because in case you are

123
00:04:35.995 --> 00:04:37.260
creating a disaster recovery strategy

124
00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.810
and you're saying, okay, my Redshift warehouse needs

125
00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.815
to be recovered into another region very quickly,

126
00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:45.600
then you know you can copy automatically your snapshots

127
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.360
across regions such as whenever a disaster

128
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.680
strikes your first region,

129
00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.713
you can restore very quickly into the other region.

130
00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.980
So how do we do a cross region snapshot copy

131
00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.930
for KMS encrypted Redshift snapshot?

132
00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.150
So you have the source and your snapshot

133
00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.020
is in your source region.

134
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.120
It's encrypted using the KMS key A,

135
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.445
and you want to copy it into destination KMS key B.

136
00:05:06.445 --> 00:05:07.800
So what we need to do

137
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.400
is call a Redshift snapshot copy grant.

138
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.520
And what this is, is that it will allow Redshift the service

139
00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.480
to perform encryption operations in the destination region.

140
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.570
When this is done, then you can copy your snapshot

141
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.270
from the region one into the region two,

142
00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.100
and then it will be encrypted by Redshift

143
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.870
with a correct KMS key.

144
00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.390
So the really magical thing here that happens

145
00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.680
is that you need to have

146
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.245
and create a snapshot copy grant.

147
00:05:34.245 --> 00:05:36.900
Redshift spectrum is a way for you

148
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.910
to query data in Amazon is free

149
00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.550
without first loading it into Redshift.

150
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.680
For this, you must have a Redshift cluster available

151
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:44.880
to start the query,

152
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.310
and then the query is then submitted to thousands

153
00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.590
of Redshift spectrum nodes.

154
00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:50.423
So how does that work?

155
00:05:50.423 --> 00:05:52.710
Well, we have our existing Redshift cluster

156
00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.440
has a leader node and has a bunch of compute nodes.

157
00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.710
But then if we do a query on data on Amazon S3

158
00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.470
that will look like this, then what's going to happen

159
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.450
is that Redshift is going to spin up a lot

160
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:07.110
of virtual spectrum nodes that will do the computation

161
00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.840
and the query of our dataset in Amazon S3.

162
00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.430
And then once the result is there, it's going

163
00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.430
to be sent back to the compute nodes for aggregation

164
00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.080
and then it will be rolled back into the leader node.

165
00:06:17.921 --> 00:06:18.990
You also have a feature

166
00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.535
called Redshift Workload Management, WLM.

167
00:06:21.535 --> 00:06:25.800
So it enables you to flexibly manage queries priorities

168
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.120
within workloads.

169
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.320
And the use case for this

170
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.770
is to prevent short fast running queries from getting stuck

171
00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.398
behind long running queries.

172
00:06:33.398 --> 00:06:35.322
You can define multiple query queues.

173
00:06:35.322 --> 00:06:38.310
You can define, for example, a super user queue,

174
00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.630
user defined queues, and so on.

175
00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.520
And then you route your query

176
00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.605
to the appropriate queue at runtime.

177
00:06:43.605 --> 00:06:45.750
So in this example, I have Amazon Redshift

178
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:46.650
and I have three queues.

179
00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.380
I have the super user queue, the short running queue,

180
00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.760
and the long running queue.

181
00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.170
The names are pretty explicit

182
00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.760
and say we have two kinds of users.

183
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.435
We have our admin and our user.

184
00:06:55.435 --> 00:06:58.170
So the admin, they want their queries to be done

185
00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.250
as soon as possible.

186
00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.410
So they will do system queries,

187
00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.940
and then these queries will go directly

188
00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.470
into the super user queue.

189
00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.120
And this will have priority,

190
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.550
the user may have some short running queries,

191
00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.960
and these we want to have

192
00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.640
into the short running query queue.

193
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.525
This way we are sure that it will just be a lot

194
00:07:13.525 --> 00:07:15.465
of short running queries,

195
00:07:15.465 --> 00:07:18.600
but if the user is submitting a longer running query

196
00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.250
and we know it's gonna take a lot of time,

197
00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:23.190
then we should send it directly into the long running queue

198
00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.083
to make sure not to block the short running queries.

199
00:07:26.083 --> 00:07:28.950
So we have two kind of workload management.

200
00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.320
The first one is automatic WLM,

201
00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.720
where the queues and resources are managed by Redshift.

202
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.570
And the second one is manual WLM, where the queues

203
00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:39.217
and resources are managed by you, the user.

204
00:07:39.217 --> 00:07:42.510
Then we have Redshift concurrency scaling.

205
00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:47.220
So this enables you to provide consistently fast performance

206
00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.590
with virtually unlimited number of users

207
00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.500
and queries by adding Redshift capacity on the fly.

208
00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.210
So this additional cluster capacity

209
00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.880
is called concurrency scaling cluster and allows you

210
00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.860
to process an increase in requests.

211
00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.690
So let's say we have a normal Redshift cluster

212
00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:01.950
with a few nodes another,

213
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.380
and then this sustains a bunch of users

214
00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:05.730
that we have normally,

215
00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.640
but say tomorrow we get a lot more users submitting queries

216
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.870
into Amazon Redshift,

217
00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.720
then automatically the concurrency scaling cluster

218
00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.720
will add nodes automatically to accommodate

219
00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.083
for this number of users.

220
00:08:17.083 --> 00:08:20.160
Now we get the ability to decide which queries get sent

221
00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.870
to the concurrency scaling cluster

222
00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.930
using the WLM feature we just saw before.

223
00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:27.544
And this feature is charge per second.

224
00:08:27.544 --> 00:08:30.360
So that's all you need to know about Redshift.

225
00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.310
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

